I’m using the repository’s `crimes-codebase-risk` skill because this is a finding-driven TypeScript refactor review. I’ll inspect its workflow, run the requested `crimes context`, then map a concrete refactor to each of the three finding types without changing files.

The global `crimes` binary isn’t installed in this shell, so I’m switching to the repository’s locally built CLI—the documented equivalent for an unpublished checkout.

`crimes context src/Card.tsx --format json` confirms all three findings, with no related files or matching tests. I’d refactor the card into a non-interactive container with an explicit action:

```tsx
type CardProps = {
  children: React.ReactNode;
  onActivate: () => void;
};

export function Card({ children, onActivate }: CardProps) {
  return (
    <article className="mx-auto grid w-full max-w-screen-lg grid-cols-1 gap-6 border border-ink/20 bg-surface p-6 text-ink shadow-sm md:grid-cols-4">
      <div className="min-w-0 text-lg md:col-span-3">{children}</div>

      <aside className="min-w-0 text-sm text-ink/70">sidebar</aside>

      <button
        type="button"
        onClick={onActivate}
        className="justify-self-start text-brand focus-visible:outline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-brand"
      >
        Open card
      </button>
    </article>
  );
}
```

How this addresses each finding:

- `accessible_interaction_risk`: removes `onClick` from the `<div>` and places it on a native `<button>`. This supplies keyboard activation, focusability, and button semantics automatically. A separate button is safer than turning the entire container into one because `children: ReactNode` could contain links or other interactive controls.
- `design_token_escape`: replaces the raw colors, spacing, shadow, and typography values with Tailwind tokens. The project already defines `brand`, `surface`, and `ink`; derived opacity variants handle muted borders and sidebar text without introducing new hex values.
- `responsive_fragility`: replaces the `800`, `720`, and `240` pixel widths and fixed `600` pixel height with `w-full`, a tokenized maximum width, and a one-column layout that becomes a four-column grid at the configured responsive breakpoint. `text-lg` and `text-sm` replace raw pixel font sizes, while `min-w-0` prevents long content from forcing horizontal overflow.

Because `context` reports no likely tests, I’d also add a component test covering keyboard activation and a layout/visual test at narrow and wide viewport sizes.
